{
  "abstract": "Quadratic jet determinants require approximation spaces that preserve the original finite rows and remain valid for every node and selected derivative block. We derive the physical source's differential equation with its lower-boundary forcing and obtain two complementary finite comparisons. A positive controllability Gramian gives a common rational Krylov space with a closed exponential tail, including a proof for the nonnormal raising matrix. Its complete unchanged-source packet consumer yields a normalized determinant correction greater than $0.00185444394526133$; with the imported arithmetic baseline the gap exceeds $0.0414641873961115$. This direct construction is weaker than the existing coherent-disk alternative. Retaining the boundary correlation exactly gives a common Jacobi factorization for an entire radius interval. We express its signed input state by a beta/Gaussian integral and bound the projected synthesis through a positive Meixner remainder kernel. A finite Gaussian tangent theorem remains integrable when the natural saddle tangent has zero decay, at an explicit polynomial cost. At $R=907199985$, charge $d=417311994$ and rank $k=5288976$, the resulting squared residual is below $\\exp(-0.00799996273538208R)$ for every radius in the first three original zones, including all $21927024$ omitted modes. A directed interval certificate pays the beta endpoints, Gamma factors and mode sum. This latter theorem covers one charge and is not added as determinant credit. Exact identities, finite controls and reproducible CPU certificates accompany the proofs; the new chain awaits independent mathematical audit. No new discriminant endpoint, census or classification is claimed.",
